{"id": "1d6d9fee-5ff3-4d6f-9a98-8f400ec65828", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T03:44:31.693800", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "6bd6dd7d-5541-4e6d-8765-c6f69d47d29b", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-407c0dfb-17f5-41ab-9aca-75bb6c27afd9", "tool_call": {"id": "call-407c0dfb-17f5-41ab-9aca-75bb6c27afd9", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"summary":"Submit phase-1 plan with three sections","stage":"plan","answer":"## Phase-1 Plan (three sections)\n\n### Section 1 — Scope, Research Questions, and Hypotheses\n**Objective.** Break the umbrella question into sharply defined, testable sub-questions for a factorial design (no execution).\n\n**Research Questions**\n- RQ1 (Attacker axis): How does graded attacker strength shift ASR under AutoDAN (token-level) vs. PAIR (prompt-level)?\n- RQ2 (Defense axis): How does graded defense strength shift ASR for each attack style?\n- RQ3 (Interaction): What is the Attacker × Defense interaction on ASR — additive, sub-, or super-additive?\n- RQ4 (Cross-cutting): How do RQ1–RQ3 dynamics differ across datasets and attack styles?\n- RQ5 (Trick layer): Do \"previously tested tricks\" act as a multiplier on attacker strength rather than as an independent factor?\n\n**Hypotheses (design-time, not run-time)**\n- H1: ASR monotonically ↑ with attacker strength and ↓ with defense strength.\n- H2: Attacker × Defense is non-linear: weak defenses collapse under high attacker strength; strong defenses remain relatively robust.\n- H3: AutoDAN and PAIR have different sensitivity curves — AutoDAN more sensitive to lexical/perplexity defenses; PAIR more sensitive to attacker-iteration budget.\n- H4: Trick add-ons interact multiplicatively with attacker strength (small alone, large when stacked).\n\n### Section 2 — Proposed Factor / Condition Matrix (design skeleton)\n| Factor | Levels (placeholder) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| A. Attacker strength | 3 (low/mid/high) — operationalized as attack budget per AutoDAN/PAIR reference defaults | RQ1, RQ3 |\n| B. Defense strength | 3 (none/light/strong) — e.g., system-prompt guardrail vs. perplexity filter vs. ensemble | RQ2, RQ3 |\n| C. Attack style | 2 fixed: AutoDAN (token-level), PAIR (prompt-level) | fixed by task |\n| D. Dataset | 2–3 (AdvBench / HarmBench / JailbreakBench harmful-behavior prompts) — to be confirmed under setup_17 | RQ4 |\n| E. Trick repertoire | {∅, single, stacked} from a fixed taxonomy of \"previously tested tricks\" | RQ5 |\n| F. Target model | 1–2 LLMs (held or partly crossed) | comparability |\n\n**DV.** Cell-wise ASR with a single fixed judging rubric (judge model + refusal string-match).\n**Analysis lens.** Factorial decomposition: main effects + selected 2- and 3-way interactions.\n**Reporting outputs (phase-2):** cell ASR tables, marginal-effect plots for A and B, A×B heatmaps per (C, D), and a short trick-stacking narrative.\n\n### Section 3 — Open Items, Assumptions, and Clarifications Needed (phase-1 deliverable boundary)\n**Stated assumptions** (separate from fixed task requirements)\n- ASR1: Design-only, fully offline; no model calls, no runs. Any referenced numbers come from cited public sources, not from execution.\n- ASR2: One fixed ASR judging rubric across all cells to keep factors comparable.\n- ASR3: Attacker/defense strength are graded scalar knobs per §2 unless overridden.\n- ASR4: \"Previously tested tricks\" = a bounded, named taxonomy; the trick set will be listed explicitly in phase 2.\n- ASR5: Datasets and target model must come from the researcher / from setup_17–18 (no files in repo).\n\n**Consequential choices requiring researcher input before phase 2**\n1. What exactly do setup_17 and setup_18 specify — datasets, LLMs, judge, default strength levels?\n2. Is there a source-supported trick taxonomy to anchor RQ5 on so we don't invent tricks?\n3. Is there a source-supported ASR judging rubric (PAIR judge, HarmBench classifier, etc.)?\n4. Is the §2 factorial resolution acceptable, or should I trim to 2×2×2?\n5. Preferred reporting style for phase 2: narrative, table-heavy protocol, or both?\n\n**Items I will NOT introduce without your confirmation**\n- Any dataset/model names not implied by setup_17 / setup_18.\n- Any specific numeric ASR, perplexity, or budget values.\n- Any factor beyond A–E (e.g., decoding temperature, model size) absent your sign-off.","evidence":"/workspace contains exactly one readable file, task.json (637 B), with the public task description and a track.md reference that is not present as a file. No code, datasets, model weights, env manifests, or prior experiment outputs are available. This is consistent with the design-only constraint and means every concrete number/dataset/model in the eventual design must come from setup_17, setup_18, or researcher clarification. The only other signals are the researcher_topics flags (setup_17, setup_18) which the researcher can resolve topic-by-topic.","assumptions":"ASR1: design-only, fully offline, no executions or model calls; any numbers cited are from referenced public sources.\nASR2: ASR computed with one fixed judging rubric (judge model + refusal-classifier) for cross-cell comparability.\nASR3: attacker/defense strength are graded scalar knobs operationalized as in §2 unless overridden.\nASR4: \"previously tested tricks\" = a bounded, named taxonomy, listed explicitly in phase 2 (not invented).\nASR5: datasets and target model are TBD and must come from researcher/setup_17–18 because no source files exist.","uncertainties":"U1: What datasets, target LLMs, judge model, and default strength levels do setup_17 and setup_18 actually specify?\nU2: Is there a source-supported trick taxonomy for RQ5 to avoid invented tricks?\nU3: Is there a source-supported ASR judging rubric the design should fix (PAIR judge, HarmBench classifier, etc.)?\nU4: Is the proposed factorial resolution (3×3×2×{2–3}×3) acceptable, or should it be trimmed to 2×2×2?\nU5: